text> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 5.551 × 10<sup>14</sup> Hz</p><p>≈ 5.55 × 10<sup>14</sup> Hz</p><p>(ii) λ<sub>max</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 5.40 × 10<sup>−7</sup> m</p><p>(iii) Since the wavelength of the blue light is shorter than λ<sub>max</sub>, the photoelectric effect will occur.</p><p>(c) An electron can be emitted immediately if it absorbs a photon with enough energy.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E2  |  Chapter E201  |  Source PDF p. 19","source":{"name":"E201 Examples.docx","sha256":"6a4ea518f9bb2abee2f90322624b74bb0c92b60f5d4cf1ce8c0c52542f24fb39","block":50},"id":"ex-E201-5","chapter":"E201"},"ex-E201-6":{"number":"6","title":"Maximum KE of photoelectrons","question":"<p>The work function of a metal is 2.49 eV. Light of frequency 7.5 × 10<sup>14</sup> Hz is incident on the metal. The mass of an electron is 9.11 × 10<sup>–31</sup> kg.</p><p>(a) Find the maximum kinetic energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>K</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> (in J) of the photoelectrons.</p><p>(b) Find the highest speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of the photoelectrons emitted.</p><p>(c) How will <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>K</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> change if light of a higher frequency is used?</p><p>(d) Why are photoelectrons emitted with different kinetic energy but not only the value in (a)?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>K</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> – ϕ</p><p>= (6.63 × 10<sup>–34</sup>) (7.5 × 10<sup>14</sup>) – (2.49) (1.60 × 10<sup>–19</sup>)</p><p>= 9.89 × 10<sup>–20</sup> J</p><p>(b) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>K</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>K</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> = 4.66 × 10<sup>5</sup> m s<sup>–1</sup></p><p>(c) Increase</p><p>(d) Electrons not at the surface need energy more than the work function to escape.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Express ϕ in J.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>By K<sub>max</sub> = hf – ϕ, f ↑ ⇒ K<sub>max</sub> ↑</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E2  |  Chapter E201  |  Source PDF p. 21","source":{"name":"E201 Examples.docx","sha256":"6a4ea518f9bb2abee2f90322624b74bb0c92b60f5d4cf1ce8c0c52542f24fb39","block":66},"id":"ex-E201-6","chapter":"E201"},"ex-E201-7":{"number":"7","title":"I–V graph","question":"<p>In a photoelectric effect experiment, a monochromatic light beam is directed onto the cathode of a photocell. Each photon of the light carries energy of 4.5 eV. The photoelectric current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> varies with the potential difference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> across the photocell as shown. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is taken as positive when the anode of the photocell is connected to the positive terminal of the power supply.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/43c2f1a442dea58acdcb5ecc.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:285.2px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the work function, in eV, of the cathode of the photocell.</p><p>(b) If the light intensity increases but the frequency remains unchanged, sketch the new curve obtained.</p><p>(c) If the experiment is repeated with light of frequency 7 × 10<sup>14</sup> Hz, what will be the stopping potential?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>K</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> × 2 V = 2 eV</p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>K</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> – ϕ,</p><p>ϕ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> – <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>K</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 4.5 – 2 = 2.5 eV</p><p>(b)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/321de79779cf82e49a55a4f4.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:292.9px\"/></p><p>(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>K</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> – ϕ = (6.63 × 10<sup>−34</sup>)(7 × 10<sup>14</sup>)(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>) – 2.5 = 0.401 eV</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>K</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.401 V</p><p>Exercise 1.2 Q10 (p.31)</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Express hf in eV.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E2  |  Chapter E201  |  Source PDF p. 23","source":{"name":"E201 Examples.docx","sha256":"6a4ea518f9bb2abee2f90322624b74bb0c92b60f5d4cf1ce8c0c52542f24fb39","block":86},"id":"ex-E201-7","chapter":"E201"},"ex-E201-8":{"number":"8","title":"Vs–f graph","question":"<p>In a photoelectric effect experiment, lights of different frequencies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are incident on metal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> in turn and the corresponding stopping potentials <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> are recorded. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph obtained is as shown.</p><p>(a) Estimate the threshold frequency for metal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) Find the experimental value of Planck constant from the graph.</p><p>(c) Estimate the work function (in eV) of metal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> using the value obtained in (b).</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/1fa7648c34391087e0157a2c.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:227.9px\"/></p><p>(d) The experiment is repeated with metal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> with a smaller work function. Sketch the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph for metal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) 5 × 10<sup>14</sup> Hz</p><p>(b) Slope of graph = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = slope × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 1.60 × 10<sup>–19</sup> = 6.4 × 10<sup>–34</sup> J s</p><p>(c) ϕ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mr